{"entries":{"chapter-8-study-principles-of-geriatric-assessment|Chapter 8 Study: Principles of Geriatric Assessment":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Extend disease-focused assessment to cognition, mood, function, frailty, social support, finances, environment, spirituality and care preferences (p117).":["u3"],"copy|Instruments support rather than replace clinical judgment. Individual clinician assessment and interdisciplinary comprehensive geriatric assessment (CGA) are related but distinct (p117).":["u4"],"copy|Tailor assessment to the setting: home visits reveal environmental risks; nursing-home assessment emphasizes nutrition and self-care rather than shopping or meal preparation (p118).":["u5"],"copy|Weight loss above 4% in the previous year prompts evaluation; obtain baseline height and weight and weigh patients at subsequent visits (p120).":["u6"],"copy|Observe walking and chair rise. Timed Up and Go uses a 3-m walk; over 12 seconds warrants further evaluation (p121).":["u7"],"copy|Cognitive screens can alter the probability of dementia but do not diagnose it; normal results do not completely exclude it (p121).":["u8"],"copy|Ask about depressed mood and anhedonia over 2 weeks; follow a positive brief screen with fuller assessment (p123).":["u9"],"copy|The chapter reports PHQ-9 above 10 with 88% sensitivity and 88% specificity; for dementia, caregiver-rated Cornell score at least 6 has 93% sensitivity and 97% specificity (p123).":["u10"],"copy|Distinguish basic self-care, instrumental independent-living tasks and advanced activities; new functional change should prompt diagnostic evaluation (p123).":["u11"],"copy|Combine reported function with observed performance when useful. Function reflects disease in the context of environment and support (p123).":["u12"],"copy|Establish who helps with specific tasks, whether help is paid and who could assist during illness. Financial strain can present as nonadherence, weight loss or apparent neglect (p124).":["u13"],"copy|CGA includes physical health, function, psychological health and socioenvironmental factors (p128).":["u14"],"copy|Its essential sequence is targeting appropriate patients, assessment with recommendations, then implementation and adherence; assessment alone does not ensure benefit (p128).":["u15"],"copy|A positive screen is a starting point for clinical evaluation, not a substitute diagnosis or an automatic drug indication (p117, p121).":["u16"],"exam-cited-book-images|Exam-cited book images":["u17"],"figure-8-2-book-image|Figure 8-2 — book image":["u18"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Figure 8-2, p.124":["u19","u33"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u20"],"copy|2024-Sep Q92":["u21"],"copy|2026-Jun Q38":["u22","u31"],"copy|CHAPTER 8s / HAZZARD":["u23"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 8 - Principles of Geriatric Assessment; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u24"],"copy|Scope of assessment":["u25"],"copy|High-yield screening":["u26"],"copy|Function & support":["u27"],"copy|Management & exam traps":["u28"],"copy|Book images cited by past exams.":["u29"],"copy|Tap an image to zoom.":["u30"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (2):":["u32"],"copy|Two past-exam questions cite printed pages in Chapter 8.":["u32"],"copy|The chapter reports PHQ-9 above 10 with 88% sensitivity and 88% specificity; for dementia, it reports 93% sensitivity and 97% specificity at a Cornell score of at least 6 (p123). In the original Cornell instrument, the clinician rates symptoms using information from the patient and an informant. Alexopoulos et al., 1988.":["u10"]},"maxId":33}
